<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The minimum a thing needs to be hauled or eaten. A real ThingDef belongs to the things line of
  work; this exists so pawns have something to pick up.
-->
<Defs>

  <!--
    900 units is 0.9 nutrition, the vanilla value of every meal class including the packaged
    ration this stands for (a-08 section 2). At 450 it was half a meal, which is why five
    colonists ate fifteen a day. The ration class never rots, which is what lets a pantry be an
    objective rather than a four-day countdown; spoilage itself is out of the slice.

    Meals stack to 20, so a whole starting pile (ScenarioDef.mealsPerPile, 12) is legal stock in
    one cell and a hauler moves it in one trip (a-14: one stack per trip). The ItemDef default of
    1 would make every pile full the moment it was hauled. The 20 is ASSUMED as a number: the
    research records limits from 1 to 500 and none for a meal.
  -->
  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_Meal</defName>
    <label>ration pack</label>
    <nutrition>900</nutrition>
    <stackLimit>20</stackLimit>
    <category>Food</category>
    <!-- Design 48 section 4: second choice after a cooked meal, never rots, and thought of as
         exactly what every food was thought of before the kitchen (Thought_AteRation, +20). -->
    <foodTier>1</foodTier>
    <ateThought>8</ateThought>
  </ItemDef>

  <!--
    Scrap metal (design 32 §14): what power lines and machines are built from, and what the
    wreckage on the board and the scrap drop pay out. It was a heap in its own right, one to a
    cell, until power gave it a use; 50 to a stack is a run of line's worth in one armful.

    Filed under Materials rather than Items: it is raw stuff, and what the reclaimer will eat.
    The defName stays Item_Salvage because an item handle is a save contract; the name a player
    reads is the registry's (ui.res.scrap, "Scrap metal").
  -->
  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_Salvage</defName>
    <label>scrap metal</label>
    <stackLimit>50</stackLimit>
    <category>Materials</category>
  </ItemDef>

  <!-- Wood's 75 is the one limit the research states outright (a-14 section 4). -->
  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_Wood</defName>
    <label>wood</label>
    <stackLimit>75</stackLimit>
    <category>Materials</category>
  </ItemDef>


  <!--
    What mining leaves on the floor. All three stack to 75 like wood: a stack is one haul, and a
    quarry that needed three trips per cell would read as punishment rather than as work.
    Nothing consumes any of them yet - the build pipeline is what gives stone a purpose.
  -->
  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_Stone</defName>
    <label>stone</label>
    <stackLimit>75</stackLimit>
    <category>Materials</category>
  </ItemDef>

  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_IronOre</defName>
    <label>iron ore</label>
    <stackLimit>75</stackLimit>
    <category>Materials</category>
  </ItemDef>

  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_Coal</defName>
    <label>coal</label>
    <stackLimit>75</stackLimit>
    <category>Materials</category>
  </ItemDef>

  <!--
    The first raw food (docs/design/22-growing.md §2). 180 units: five carrots are 900, one
    sowing's yield is one meal-equivalent, and a colonist burning 1,600 a day is fed by about
    seven field tiles. Eaten straight, like a ration pack - the eater scans for nutrition and
    does not care that nothing cooked it. Stacks to 40, so one harvest fits beside any stack
    already in the cell and a hauler clears a tile in one trip.
  -->
  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_Carrots</defName>
    <label>carrots</label>
    <nutrition>180</nutrition>
    <stackLimit>75</stackLimit>
    <category>Food</category>
    <!-- Design 48 section 4: raw food, so a cook can put three in a pan (540 of the meal's 500),
         and a colonist eating them straight thinks less of it (Thought_AteRaw). -->
    <foodTier>3</foodTier>
    <rawIngredient>true</rawIngredient>
    <ateThought>10</ateThought>
  </ItemDef>

  <!--
    The wild foods (design 45 section 6), both raw and eaten straight like carrots. INVENTED:
    a picking of eight berries at 60 is 480, about half a meal; a find of mushrooms, three to five
    at 70, is a snack. Both stack to 75 like every other raw commodity.
  -->
  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_Berries</defName>
    <label>berries</label>
    <nutrition>60</nutrition>
    <stackLimit>75</stackLimit>
    <category>Food</category>
    <!-- Raw food, like the carrots (design 48 section 4): a cook can put them in a pan, and eaten
         straight they are thought of as raw. Without these a wild food would default to tier 0
         and be taken before a cooked meal. -->
    <foodTier>3</foodTier>
    <rawIngredient>true</rawIngredient>
    <ateThought>10</ateThought>
  </ItemDef>

  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_Mushrooms</defName>
    <label>mushrooms</label>
    <nutrition>70</nutrition>
    <stackLimit>75</stackLimit>
    <category>Food</category>
    <!-- Raw food, like the carrots (design 48 section 4): a cook can put them in a pan, and eaten
         straight they are thought of as raw. Without these a wild food would default to tier 0
         and be taken before a cooked meal. -->
    <foodTier>3</foodTier>
    <rawIngredient>true</rawIngredient>
    <ateThought>10</ateThought>
  </ItemDef>

  <!--
    The four melee weapons (design 33 section 1, C3), the owner's four: a bat and a crowbar, blunt
    with a chance to stun, and a machete and a sci-fi blade, sharp. Real items, one to a stack,
    filed under Weapons, appended together by the combat contracts step. The names are our own
    (docs/design/icon-keys.csv); nothing spawns one yet, and the starting kit is lane D's.

    The owner set the envelope and the rest is INVENTED inside it, to be tuned after the first
    play: weapons do 7 to 10 a blow every 1.6 to 2.4 seconds, against bare hands' 4 every 2
    (Combat.xml). Cooldowns are ticks at sixty a second. Blunt trades speed for the stun; sharp
    is quicker, and the arc blade is simply the best thing a colonist can hold.

    windupTicks is when the blow lands after the swing starts, and it is the number the drawn
    clip is timed to: the Sword Combat pack's light swings reach their impact about 0.37 s in and
    its heavy ones about 1.0 s in (docs/research/synty-sword-combat.md), played faster or slower to
    land on this tick. style picks the family of clip, never a clip.

    experiencePerSwing is in thousandths of a point, the SkillDef unit, landed or not: 50 points a
    swing, so a minute of fighting is worth about a working day's cap. INVENTED; the reference only
    says melee trains fast.
  -->
  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_Bat</defName>
    <label>bat</label>
    <category>Weapons</category>
    <weapon>
      <damage>7</damage>
      <cooldownTicks>120</cooldownTicks>
      <windupTicks>30</windupTicks>
      <damageKind>Blunt</damageKind>
      <stunPerMille>200</stunPerMille>
      <stunTicks>60</stunTicks>
      <experiencePerSwing>50000</experiencePerSwing>
      <style>Heavy</style>
    </weapon>
  </ItemDef>

  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_Crowbar</defName>
    <label>crowbar</label>
    <category>Weapons</category>
    <weapon>
      <damage>8</damage>
      <cooldownTicks>132</cooldownTicks>
      <windupTicks>36</windupTicks>
      <damageKind>Blunt</damageKind>
      <stunPerMille>250</stunPerMille>
      <stunTicks>90</stunTicks>
      <experiencePerSwing>50000</experiencePerSwing>
      <style>Heavy</style>
    </weapon>
  </ItemDef>

  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_Machete</defName>
    <label>machete</label>
    <category>Weapons</category>
    <weapon>
      <damage>8</damage>
      <cooldownTicks>96</cooldownTicks>
      <windupTicks>22</windupTicks>
      <damageKind>Sharp</damageKind>
      <experiencePerSwing>50000</experiencePerSwing>
      <style>Light</style>
    </weapon>
  </ItemDef>

  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_ArcBlade</defName>
    <label>arc blade</label>
    <category>Weapons</category>
    <weapon>
      <damage>10</damage>
      <cooldownTicks>114</cooldownTicks>
      <windupTicks>26</windupTicks>
      <damageKind>Sharp</damageKind>
      <experiencePerSwing>50000</experiencePerSwing>
      <style>Light</style>
    </weapon>
  </ItemDef>

  <!--
    Medical supplies (design 37): what a doctor treats with. One unit is one treatment, and it
    restores healPerUnit (40, the owner's number) under the treatment cap in Combat.xml's
    MedicalDef. Ten to a stack, so a stack is one shelf bay and a starting kit of six is one pile.
    The label a player reads is the registry's (ui.res.medkit, "Medical supplies").
  -->
  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_MedicalSupplies</defName>
    <label>medical supplies</label>
    <stackLimit>10</stackLimit>
    <category>Medicine</category>
    <healPerUnit>40</healPerUnit>
  </ItemDef>

  <!--
    The kitchen (design 48 section 4). A cooked meal is worth what a ration pack is (900, a-18's
    simple meal) and is the best thing to eat: tier 0, and Thought_AteMeal. Whether there was
    meat in the pan decides which of the two comes out; they are two defs rather than one with a
    memory of its ingredients, so a stack is still one def and nothing about stacking changes.
    Ten to a stack, a pot's worth, so a shelf bay holds a day's cooking for five.

    ticksToRot 240000 is four days (a-19): declared now, read from the cold store (K2) on.

    The burnt meal (owner, interview #4): edible, less filling (700, INVENTED), and a colonist who
    eats one minds (Thought_AteBurnt). Tier 2, under the ration pack and over raw food.
  -->
  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_CookedMeal</defName>
    <label>meal</label>
    <nutrition>900</nutrition>
    <stackLimit>10</stackLimit>
    <category>Food</category>
    <foodTier>0</foodTier>
    <ticksToRot>240000</ticksToRot>
    <ateThought>1</ateThought>
  </ItemDef>

  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_VegetableMeal</defName>
    <label>vegetable meal</label>
    <nutrition>900</nutrition>
    <stackLimit>10</stackLimit>
    <category>Food</category>
    <foodTier>0</foodTier>
    <ticksToRot>240000</ticksToRot>
    <ateThought>1</ateThought>
  </ItemDef>

  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_BurntMeal</defName>
    <label>burnt meal</label>
    <nutrition>700</nutrition>
    <stackLimit>10</stackLimit>
    <category>Food</category>
    <foodTier>2</foodTier>
    <ticksToRot>240000</ticksToRot>
    <ateThought>9</ateThought>
  </ItemDef>

  <!--
    The pistol (design 47 section 3b), labelled Pistol on every screen (ui.item.pistol). For a
    gun windupTicks is the AIM and cooldownTicks the cooldown, counted from the aim's start.
    Damage 10 and the one-second cooldown are the reference autopistol's. The aim is INVENTED:
    30 ticks against the reference's 18, raised so the aim is seen. Experience is a shot's,
    hit or miss, into Shooting. The range is the reference's 25.9 cells kept in metres; the
    bullet's 1,000 mm a tick (60 m/s) is INVENTED - slow enough to see and to step out of.
    The accuracy bands are at the reference autopistol's touch / short / medium / long distances,
    in metres, RAISED 2026-09-25 with the per-cell curve on the owner's first play: 95 / 85 / 65 /
    45 per cent where the reference has 80 / 70 / 40 / 30.
  -->
  <ItemDef>
    <defName>Item_Pistol</defName>
    <label>pistol</label>
    <category>Weapons</category>
    <weapon>
      <damage>10</damage>
      <cooldownTicks>60</cooldownTicks>
      <windupTicks>30</windupTicks>
      <damageKind>Bullet</damageKind>
      <experiencePerSwing>50000</experiencePerSwing>
      <style>Pistol</style>
      <ranged>
        <rangeMm>26000</rangeMm>
        <speedMmPerTick>1000</speedMmPerTick>
        <accuracyByDistance>
          <li><mm>3000</mm><perMille>950</perMille></li>
          <li><mm>12000</mm><perMille>850</perMille></li>
          <li><mm>25000</mm><perMille>650</perMille></li>
          <li><mm>40000</mm><perMille>450</perMille></li>
        </accuracyByDistance>
        <!--
          The gun's blow (design 47 section 12, the reference's rule): an enemy within reach is
          clubbed with the pistol, never shot. Blunt, 5 damage on the fists' two-second cadence, on
          the Melee skill - a little better than a bare fist and far worse than a machete, so a
          melee rush is the counter to a lone gunman. INVENTED; the quality tiers apply.
        -->
        <melee>
          <damage>5</damage>
          <cooldownTicks>120</cooldownTicks>
          <windupTicks>20</windupTicks>
          <damageKind>Blunt</damageKind>
          <experiencePerSwing>50000</experiencePerSwing>
          <style>Fists</style>
        </melee>
      </ranged>
    </weapon>
  </ItemDef>

</Defs>
